1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,410
Hello and welcome to episode
three of the Articulate Journal.

2
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:10,705
Before starting this journal, I'd
already built a couple of proof

3
00:00:10,745 --> 00:00:13,135
of concepts. They
explored different ideas.

4
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:16,440
One, I experimented with different
conversational interfaces.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,640
Another looked at architectural discovery.

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:23,905
Others explored RAG, knowledge
graphs and speech services.

7
00:00:23,945 --> 00:00:27,480
None of them became the system
that you'll see throughout this

8
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,911
journal. But they were valuable
because they taught me something.

9
00:00:31,951 --> 00:00:36,320
Every prototype answered a few
questions and raised even more.

10
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,440
This journal begins after
those early experiments.

11
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:42,960
It isn't starting from
a blank piece of paper.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,924
It's starting from a collection of
ideas, observations and architectural

13
00:00:45,964 --> 00:00:48,640
questions that I now
want to explore properly.

14
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,926
One of the biggest questions
started to emerge from all those

15
00:00:52,966 --> 00:00:56,880
experiments was this. Am I building
software that happens to use

16
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,440
AI or am I building something
fundamentally different?

17
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,760
I don't think I've got the
complete answer yet, but I

18
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,000
do think it's worth exploring.

19
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:09,250
One principle you'll hear repeatedly
throughout this journal is that I

20
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:13,171
tried to begin with a problem
rather than the technology.

21
00:01:13,211 --> 00:01:17,756
I don't start by asking
should I use agents, should I

22
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:20,796
use this framework or
what model should I call?

23
00:01:20,836 --> 00:01:24,067
Instead, what problem am I
actually trying to solve?

24
00:01:24,107 --> 00:01:29,053
The technologies come later. When I
began designing Articulate, I wasn't

25
00:01:29,093 --> 00:01:31,539
trying to build an AI-native platform.

26
00:01:31,579 --> 00:01:35,880
I was trying to build something
that could help architects discover,

27
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:37,994
understand and reason about architecture.

28
00:01:38,034 --> 00:01:41,840
That problem led me down
to some interesting paths.

29
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,069
As I explored the different approaches,
I kept finding myself facing

30
00:01:47,109 --> 00:01:51,752
the same architectural decision. I could
build another deterministic workflow,

31
00:01:51,792 --> 00:01:55,583
or I could allow a language
model to tackle the problem

32
00:01:55,623 --> 00:01:58,743
instead. At first, those
decisions seemed isolated.

33
00:01:58,783 --> 00:02:02,989
A language model could interpret
unstructured information more naturally

34
00:02:03,029 --> 00:02:08,339
than I could code. It could also
ask follow-up questions instead

35
00:02:08,379 --> 00:02:11,747
of forcing users through a
rigid sequence of screens.

36
00:02:11,787 --> 00:02:15,340
It could reason across incomplete
information instead of expecting every

37
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:17,760
input to fit into a predefined structure.

38
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:22,870
In those situations, using an LLM
wasn't simply more interesting.

39
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:26,051
It often felt like a better
architectural choice.

40
00:02:26,091 --> 00:02:30,509
The surprising part was is it
just kept happening, not once

41
00:02:30,549 --> 00:02:33,680
or twice, but across completely
different parts of the system.

42
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,575
As more of those decisions accumulated,
I noticed something else changing.

43
00:02:39,615 --> 00:02:43,650
I was no longer thinking about
AI as another integration.

44
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:48,600
Instead, I found myself thinking
about questions that as an architect,

45
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,176
I wouldn't normally ask. Like,
how should prompts be versioned?

46
00:02:52,216 --> 00:02:55,907
How do I replace one model with
another without affecting the

47
00:02:55,947 --> 00:02:59,280
rest of the architecture?
How do I know whether a newer

48
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:01,370
model is actually
producing better outcomes?

49
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:04,880
How do I detect when
behaviour starts to drift?

50
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:07,949
And how should intelligent
behaviour be evaluated?

51
00:03:07,989 --> 00:03:12,474
How could I observe and govern
reasoning in production?

52
00:03:12,514 --> 00:03:16,535
These weren't implementation details.
They were architectural concerns.

53
00:03:16,575 --> 00:03:19,120
That was an important shift.

54
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:24,646
I wasn't yet concluding that
Articulate was AI-native, but I was

55
00:03:24,686 --> 00:03:28,498
beginning to suspect that intelligence
deserved much greater architectural

56
00:03:28,538 --> 00:03:30,720
significance than I originally expected.

57
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:35,680
This journal is, in many ways,
an exploration into that idea.

58
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,360
Over the past few years,
almost lots of products on the

59
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,800
market have become AI-powered. Usually
that meant by an existing application

60
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,960
that's been enhanced
with a language model.

61
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,469
Absolutely nothing wrong with that.

62
00:03:51,509 --> 00:03:54,760
In many cases, it's exactly the
right architectural decision.

63
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:57,194
The application remains
fundamentally the same.

64
00:03:57,234 --> 00:04:00,480
AI simply makes one part of it better.

65
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:04,320
But I'm increasingly interested
in a different question.

66
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:08,880
What happens when intelligence is
no longer just another feature?

67
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,640
What happens when it starts to
influence the architecture itself?

68
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:17,492
That's the distinction I currently make.

69
00:04:17,532 --> 00:04:20,569
An AI-powered application uses AI.

70
00:04:20,609 --> 00:04:25,924
An AI-native application is designed
around intelligence as one of its

71
00:04:25,964 --> 00:04:30,301
architectural capabilities. Whether
Articulate truly deserves that label is

72
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:34,840
something I expect this journal
to test rather than simply assume.

73
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:38,913
If intelligence is becoming
architecturally significant, then it

74
00:04:38,953 --> 00:04:42,160
begins to introduce capabilities that
traditional software rarely considers.

75
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:48,280
The runtime may need to understand
intent, build and maintain context,

76
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:53,713
retrieve relevant knowledge, reason
across incomplete information, plan work,

77
00:04:53,753 --> 00:04:58,376
invoke tools, evaluate confidence,
and collaborate with people.

78
00:04:58,416 --> 00:05:02,653
Those capabilities don't replace
traditional software engineering.

79
00:05:02,693 --> 00:05:08,088
The system needs APIs, persistence,
security, messaging, reliability, and

80
00:05:08,128 --> 00:05:12,595
observability. The difference is that
intelligence begins to sit alongside

81
00:05:12,635 --> 00:05:14,665
those capabilities rather than on top.

82
00:05:14,705 --> 00:05:18,433
That's a very different way of
thinking about architecture.

83
00:05:18,473 --> 00:05:24,560
Another term that keeps constantly
appearing is agentic systems.

84
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:28,069
And although these two ideas are
related, I don't actually think

85
00:05:28,109 --> 00:05:33,468
they're the same. Agentic systems
describe one way of organising intelligent

86
00:05:33,508 --> 00:05:38,766
behaviour. An AI-native system may contain
one agent, many specialised agents,

87
00:05:38,806 --> 00:05:42,080
or perhaps no explicit agents at all.

88
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,754
The number of agents
isn't what interests me.

89
00:05:45,794 --> 00:05:47,920
The architectural
question is much simpler.

90
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:53,145
Which responsibilities belong
to intelligence and which remain

91
00:05:53,185 --> 00:05:57,440
deterministic? That's the question I
want to explore throughout this project.

92
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:01,224
I'll return to agents later in
a series when I've established

93
00:06:01,264 --> 00:06:03,840
enough architectural foundations
to discuss them properly.

94
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:08,994
Articulate isn't intended to use
AI simply because it's because AI

95
00:06:09,034 --> 00:06:14,126
is fashionable. Every significant
architectural decision should solve a real

96
00:06:14,166 --> 00:06:18,274
problem. If a deterministic solution
is simpler, more reliable, and easier

97
00:06:18,314 --> 00:06:20,640
to understand, that's
probably the right choice.

98
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:25,313
But where the problem involves
interpretation, uncertainty, incomplete

99
00:06:25,353 --> 00:06:29,422
knowledge, or architectural reasoning,
my early experiments consistently

100
00:06:29,462 --> 00:06:33,406
suggested that intelligence offered
a better architectural approach.

101
00:06:33,446 --> 00:06:38,280
Whether that observation continues to
hold true is something this journal

102
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:42,867
will test openly. Some ideas
may survive, others may not.

103
00:06:42,907 --> 00:06:47,827
The important thing is that the
architectural reasoning remains visible.

104
00:06:47,867 --> 00:06:53,300
If intelligence really is becoming an
architectural capability, then it raises

105
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:55,910
an entirely new set of questions.

106
00:06:55,950 --> 00:06:58,560
What is context? What is memory?

107
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:03,949
Where should knowledge live? How
should reasoning be coordinated?

108
00:07:03,989 --> 00:07:06,945
How should long-running work be managed?

109
00:07:06,985 --> 00:07:10,770
How do humans remain part of
those important decisions?

110
00:07:10,810 --> 00:07:13,360
How do we evaluate intelligent behaviour?

111
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:18,160
Those questions don't have
obvious answers right now.

112
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:23,360
They're exactly the questions I want
to investigate as Articulate evolves.

113
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,588
So rather than treating AI-native
architecture as a conclusion, I would

114
00:07:27,628 --> 00:07:30,047
like to treat it as a hypothesis.

115
00:07:30,087 --> 00:07:36,320
One that I'll explore further
throughout the rest of this journal.
